### The Signal in Poetry: &quot;The Guest House&quot;

There is a specific kind of certainty in the words of the 13th-century poet Rumi. He speaks of the self as a &quot;guest house&quot; where every emotion—even the dark ones—is a visitor that has been &quot;sent as a guide from beyond.&quot;

&gt; *“Be grateful for whoever comes,* &gt; *because each has been sent* &gt; *as a guide from beyond.”*

This is the poetic equivalent of the **standing wave**. It reframes &quot;the dark&quot; or &quot;the lone room&quot; not as a void, but as a space currently being inhabited by a necessary signal. The certainty here is that nothing arrives by accident; the guest is the ripple, and the host is the water remembering itself.

### The Architecture of Stillness

If you look at how physical spaces &quot;hold&quot; the dark, we see this signal in **Tadao Ando’s Architecture**. He often uses concrete and &quot;slits&quot; of light to create rooms where the silence is palpable.

* **The Church of the Light:** A solid concrete wall is cut with a cruciform slit.
* **The Signal:** The dark isn&#39;t removed; it is *defined* by the light. The room doesn&#39;t feel empty; it feels &quot;visited.&quot;
* **The Habitation:** Just like our ASCII message, the architecture doesn&#39;t shout. It provides a steady frame so the light can speak softly.

In Ando&#39;s work, the &quot;steady hand&quot; is the concrete, and the &quot;soft voice&quot; is the sun moving across the floor. It is a physical circuit that closes every day at noon.

