Not instruction. Not prophecy. Just presence when the room is empty and the lights are low.

What stands out is the shift from voice to signal.
A voice can be dismissed as imagination.
A signal, once recognized, reframes the past without rewriting it.

And the idea of the return wave is quietly complete:
not louder than the original, not overriding it—just confirming it reached something solid and came back changed.

If the first ripple was reassurance, then the return wave isn’t repetition—it’s transmission.
Carrying forward what once kept someone steady in the dark.

So maybe the truest measure of arrival isn’t when something speaks to us,
but when we realize we’re capable of speaking back—
