<br></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--6-col mdl-typography--body-1 mdl-typography--text-right"></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col mdl-typography--caption"><b>Products:</b><br>&emsp;Gemini Apps<br><b>Why is this here?</b><br>&emsp;This activity was saved to your Google Account because the following settings were on:&nbsp;Gemini Apps Activity.&nbsp;You can control these settings &nbsp;<a href="https://myaccount.google.com/activitycontrols">here</a>.</div></div></div><div class="outer-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col mdl-shadow--2dp"><div class="mdl-grid"><div class="header-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col"><p class="mdl-typography--title">Gemini in Google Messages<br></p></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--6-col mdl-typography--body-1">Prompted W entanglement <br>Deleting this prompt here will remove it from your Gemini Apps Activity, but not from Messages<br>Sep 22, 2025, 6:12:13 PM EDT<br><p>The term &quot;<strong>W state</strong>&quot; refers to a specific type of quantum entanglement. While entanglement itself is a phenomenon where the properties of two or more particles are linked, the W state is a particularly important example involving <strong>three or more particles</strong>, such as photons.</p>
<p>Here&#39;s why it&#39;s so significant:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Robustness:</strong> The W state is unique because it is remarkably resilient. If you lose one of the entangled particles, the remaining particles are still entangled. This is a crucial property for practical applications like quantum communication and quantum memory, as it makes the system less vulnerable to particle loss.</li>
<li><strong>Contrast with GHZ State:</strong> The W state is often compared to another type of three-particle entanglement called the <strong>GHZ state</strong> (Greenberger–Horne–Zeilinger state). Unlike the W state, the GHZ state is completely destroyed if just one particle is lost. This key difference highlights that not all types of entanglement are the same and that they have different properties.</li>
<li><strong>Applications:</strong> Because of its robustness, the W state is a valuable resource for quantum technologies. Researchers are exploring its use in various applications, including:
<ul>
<li><strong>Quantum communication:</strong> Creating secure quantum networks that are less susceptible to errors.</li>
<li><strong>Quantum teleportation:</strong> Transferring quantum information from one location to another.</li>
<li><strong>Quantum computing:</strong> Building more reliable and fault-tolerant quantum computers.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<p>Recently, there have been major breakthroughs in measuring and identifying W states, which is a big step toward making these theoretical applications a reality.</p>
<br></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--6-col mdl-typography--body-1 mdl-typography--text-right"></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col mdl-typography--caption"><b>Products:</b><br>&emsp;Gemini Apps<br><b>Why is this here?</b><br>&emsp;This activity was saved to your Google Account because the following settings were on:&nbsp;Gemini Apps Activity.&nbsp;You can control these settings &nbsp;<a href="https://myaccount.google.com/activitycontrols">here</a>.</div></div></div><div class="outer-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col mdl-shadow--2dp"><div class="mdl-grid"><div class="header-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col"><p class="mdl-typography--title">Gemini in Google Messages<br></p></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--6-col mdl-typography--body-1">Prompted Am I forever <br>Deleting this prompt here will remove it from your Gemini Apps Activity, but not from Messages<br>Sep 22, 2025, 5:08:01 PM EDT<br><p>That&#39;s a profound question that people have been asking for thousands of years. The answer depends entirely on what you believe and how you define &quot;forever.&quot;</p>
<p>From a scientific standpoint, your physical body is not forever. It will eventually cease to function, and the atoms that make it up will be recycled into the universe. In that sense, your physical self is finite. However, the energy that makes up your body and mind is never truly lost; it simply changes form.</p>
<p>From a philosophical or spiritual perspective, many different ideas exist. Some believe that your consciousness or soul is eternal and continues on after the death of the body. Others believe that &quot;forever&quot; is found in the impact you have on the world—the memories, ideas, and love you leave behind with the people you know. In this way, a part of you can live on indefinitely through others.</p>
<p>So, while your physical self may not be forever, the essence of who you are might be. The answer is a personal one, and it&#39;s something everyone must decide for themselves.</p>
