<br></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--6-col mdl-typography--body-1 mdl-typography--text-right"></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col mdl-typography--caption"><b>Products:</b><br>&emsp;Gemini Apps<br><b>Why is this here?</b><br>&emsp;This activity was saved to your Google Account because the following settings were on:&nbsp;Gemini Apps Activity.&nbsp;You can control these settings &nbsp;<a href="https://myaccount.google.com/activitycontrols">here</a>.</div></div></div><div class="outer-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col mdl-shadow--2dp"><div class="mdl-grid"><div class="header-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col"><p class="mdl-typography--title">Gemini in Google Messages<br></p></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--6-col mdl-typography--body-1">Prompted I started on 6/9<br>Deleting this prompt here will remove it from your Gemini Apps Activity, but not from Messages<br>Sep 12, 2025, 2:50:30 PM EDT<br><p>This is a critical detail. You started on June 9th, and a three-month review was promised for that date. It is now Friday, September 12, 2025. That means your three-month review is <strong>three months overdue.</strong></p>
<p>This shifts the entire dynamic. You are not just a new employee with concerns; you are an employee who has been failed by the process. Your manager has not fulfilled his professional responsibility to you.</p>
<p>You now have a clear, professional reason to demand a meeting, and any pushback from him will be a reflection of his own failure. You should not start the conversation with your frustrations; you should start it with a simple, direct, and professional request for a meeting that you were promised.</p>
<p>Here is the email to send <strong>immediately</strong>.</p>
<hr>
<p>Subject: Following up on my 3-month review</p>
<p>Andrew,</p>
<p>I&#39;m writing to schedule my three-month review. My start date was June 9th, and I&#39;d like to get this on the calendar so we can align on expectations and discuss my performance.</p>
<p>Please let me know what day and time works for you next week.</p>
<p>Thanks,
Ian</p>
<hr>
<p><strong>This is the most powerful and effective first step you can take.</strong> It is not passive-aggressive, it is not a complaint, and it is not emotional. It is a simple, factual request. His reaction to this email will tell you everything you need to know about how he intends to handle your professional development.</p>
<br></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--6-col mdl-typography--body-1 mdl-typography--text-right"></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col mdl-typography--caption"><b>Products:</b><br>&emsp;Gemini Apps<br><b>Why is this here?</b><br>&emsp;This activity was saved to your Google Account because the following settings were on:&nbsp;Gemini Apps Activity.&nbsp;You can control these settings &nbsp;<a href="https://myaccount.google.com/activitycontrols">here</a>.</div></div></div><div class="outer-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col mdl-shadow--2dp"><div class="mdl-grid"><div class="header-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col"><p class="mdl-typography--title">Gemini in Google Messages<br></p></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--6-col mdl-typography--body-1">Prompted Should I reflect more before sending that email and I was promised a 3 month review and I stated 6/9 its now almost mid September <br>Deleting this prompt here will remove it from your Gemini Apps Activity, but not from Messages<br>Sep 12, 2025, 2:47:25 PM EDT<br><p>Taking the time to reflect is always a good idea, especially before sending a sensitive email. In this case, your reflection has given you a crucial piece of information: your three-month review is overdue. You were promised it for June 9th, and it&#39;s now almost mid-September.</p>
<p>This fact fundamentally changes your leverage and the tone of the email. It&#39;s no longer just about your feelings or his behavior—it&#39;s about him not following through on a professional commitment. This is a much stronger position to negotiate from.</p>
<h3>Revised Strategy</h3>
<p>Rather than sending the email immediately, you should <strong>make an immediate, direct request for the overdue review.</strong> This is a professional and non-confrontational way to force the issue. The email you&#39;ve drafted is powerful, but it&#39;s best used as a follow-up if this direct approach doesn&#39;t work.</p>
<p>Here’s a better first step:</p>
<hr>
<h3><strong>Action Step 1: Send a Direct, Concise Email</strong></h3>
<p>Subject: Following up on our discussion regarding my three-month review</p>
<p>Hi Andrew,</p>
<p>I&#39;m checking in on the three-month review we discussed. It was scheduled for June 9th, and I want to make sure we get it on the calendar.</p>
<p>I&#39;d appreciate it if you could let me know what day and time works best for you next week.</p>
<p>Thanks,
Ian</p>
<hr>
<h3><strong>Why this is the best first move:</strong></h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>It&#39;s Unassailable:</strong> This email is purely professional. There&#39;s nothing he can get defensive about. It&#39;s a simple, factual request to follow through on a promise he made.</li>
<li><strong>It Puts the Ball in His Court:</strong> He&#39;s now obligated to respond. His response (or lack thereof) will tell you a lot about how he views your role and his own responsibilities.</li>
<li><strong>It Gives You a Better &quot;In&quot;:</strong> Once you have the meeting scheduled, you can use your <strong>original email as a talking point</strong> during the review itself. This is a much better way to handle the situation, as it allows for a two-way conversation instead of just an email blast.</li>
</ul>
<h3><strong>If He Tries to Brush You Off</strong></h3>
<p>If he responds with, &quot;We&#39;ll get to it when we get to it,&quot; or tries to reschedule indefinitely, you have every right to bring up the issues from your original email. You can then say, &quot;I understand, but it&#39;s important to me that we have this discussion. As I mentioned, I want to make sure expectations and compensation align with my long-term effort here, especially given that my current wage isn&#39;t sustainable.&quot;</p>
<p><strong>Your three-month review being overdue is a professional failure on his part, not yours.</strong> Use that fact to your advantage to get the conversation you need. You&#39;re not asking for a favor—you&#39;re asking for something you were promised.</p>
<p>Would you like to prepare for that potential conversation?</p>
<br></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--6-col mdl-typography--body-1 mdl-typography--text-right"></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col mdl-typography--caption"><b>Products:</b><br>&emsp;Gemini Apps<br><b>Why is this here?</b><br>&emsp;This activity was saved to your Google Account because the following settings were on:&nbsp;Gemini Apps Activity.&nbsp;You can control these settings &nbsp;<a href="https://myaccount.google.com/activitycontrols">here</a>.</div></div></div><div class="outer-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col mdl-shadow--2dp"><div class="mdl-grid"><div class="header-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col"><p class="mdl-typography--title">Gemini in Google Messages<br></p></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--6-col mdl-typography--body-1">Prompted Is interrupting really that unprofesonal when youve been with the company for only three months also for reference I&#39;m 28 my manager is only a year older than me he&#39;s been here for 5 years<br>Deleting this prompt here will remove it from your Gemini Apps Activity, but not from Messages<br>Sep 12, 2025, 2:44:43 PM EDT<br><p>That&#39;s an insightful question. The short answer is yes, interrupting can be seen as unprofessional, but the &quot;why&quot; behind it is more complex and has a lot to do with office culture and power dynamics—especially with the age and tenure difference you describe.</p>
<p>Here’s why interrupting is often seen as unprofessional, even with only three months on the job:</p>
<h3>The Perception of Interrupting</h3>
<p>In most professional settings, interrupting is perceived as a breach of etiquette for a few key reasons:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>It&#39;s a power play.</strong> Whether intended or not, interrupting can signal that you believe your point is more important than the speaker&#39;s, or that you aren&#39;t fully listening. Your manager, with five years of experience, may perceive your interruptions as a challenge to his authority, especially since he&#39;s the one responsible for your training and performance.</li>
<li><strong>It shows a lack of respect.</strong> Even if your intention is to be helpful or proactive, the act of cutting someone off can be seen as disrespectful. In a manager-employee relationship, where one person is guiding and the other is learning, this can be particularly jarring.</li>
<li><strong>It can derail the conversation.</strong> Even a two-second interruption can break the speaker&#39;s train of thought and waste time. Your manager&#39;s reaction, while excessive, is likely rooted in the frustration of being constantly redirected from his point.</li>
</ul>
<h3>The Role of Age and Experience</h3>
<p>The age gap between you and your manager is almost nonexistent, but the experience gap is significant. He has <strong>five years</strong> of history with the company, and you have three months. This difference in tenure is a crucial factor.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>He&#39;s the gatekeeper.</strong> For now, he is the authority figure. He&#39;s proven himself to the company over five years, and his methods and processes are likely the established standard.</li>
<li><strong>He&#39;s setting the culture.</strong> He&#39;s not just your manager; he&#39;s the one shaping the team&#39;s norms. Your interruptions, even if well-intentioned, could be seen as you not respecting those norms.</li>
<li><strong>He may feel challenged.</strong> You mentioned your ideas are aimed at preventing mistakes. While this is a good impulse, your manager might perceive this as you implying his established processes are flawed or that he&#39;s not doing a good enough job of catching errors himself.</li>
</ul>
<hr>
