<hr>
<h3>How to Go About This</h3>
<p><strong>Is Email the Way?</strong></p>
<p>Yes, in this situation, email is the right first step. It gives you space to clearly articulate your thoughts without being interrupted or getting flustered. It also creates a written record of your concerns and sets a clear agenda for a future conversation.</p>
<p><strong>How to Respond if He Mentions the &quot;Gold Star Week&quot;</strong></p>
<p>Your best response is to stay calm and redirect the conversation back to your professional goals. Don&#39;t engage with the sarcastic language.</p>
<p>If he says, &quot;You haven&#39;t had a gold star week yet,&quot; you could respond with:</p>
<ul>
<li>&quot;I understand what you&#39;re saying, but I&#39;d like to get more specific about the metrics for a &#39;gold star week.&#39; I&#39;m committed to doing high-quality work, so I&#39;d appreciate it if we could discuss the clear expectations for that, and how it ties into my overall performance.&quot;</li>
<li>&quot;My goal is to provide consistent, high-quality work, not just one standout week. That’s why I brought up the issue of fatigue and compensation in my email. I want to make sure I can do my best work for the long term.&quot;</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Your &quot;Gold Star Week&quot;</strong></p>
<p>Your own definition of a &quot;gold star week&quot; is an important point to remember. You&#39;re right—it&#39;s about not engaging in unproductive arguments. You can say something like, &quot;I&#39;m not focused on winning points or avoiding conflict. My gold star week is one where I&#39;m able to use my full energy and focus to do great work. That&#39;s what I&#39;m hoping we can talk about.&quot;</p>
<p>Ultimately, the goal is to shift the conversation from a power struggle or a test of wills to a professional discussion about expectations, compensation, and your ability to be a high-performing employee. The first email sets you up perfectly to have that discussion.</p>
<br></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--6-col mdl-typography--body-1 mdl-typography--text-right"></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col mdl-typography--caption"><b>Products:</b><br>&emsp;Gemini Apps<br><b>Why is this here?</b><br>&emsp;This activity was saved to your Google Account because the following settings were on:&nbsp;Gemini Apps Activity.&nbsp;You can control these settings &nbsp;<a href="https://myaccount.google.com/activitycontrols">here</a>.</div></div></div><div class="outer-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col mdl-shadow--2dp"><div class="mdl-grid"><div class="header-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col"><p class="mdl-typography--title">Gemini in Google Messages<br></p></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--6-col mdl-typography--body-1">Prompted Rewrite so it won&#39;t be blocked by a content security policy <br>Deleting this prompt here will remove it from your Gemini Apps Activity, but not from Messages<br>Sep 11, 2025, 8:07:13 AM EDT<br><p>Unfortunately, there is no reliable way to rewrite a bookmarklet to guarantee it will bypass a website&#39;s Content Security Policy (CSP). The very purpose of a strict CSP is to prevent the kind of &quot;unsafe inline&quot; scripts that bookmarklets represent.</p>
<p>Here&#39;s why and what you can do instead:</p>
<h3>Why It Can&#39;t Be &quot;Rewritten&quot;</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>CSP is a Server-Side Policy:</strong> The CSP is an HTTP header sent by the website&#39;s server. It&#39;s a rulebook that the browser must follow. Your bookmarklet is an &quot;inline script,&quot; and the CSP is explicitly designed to block these scripts unless the policy includes <code>&#39;unsafe-inline&#39;</code>.</li>
<li><strong>The Browser is Enforcing the Rules:</strong> The browser, not the bookmarklet, is responsible for enforcing the CSP. When you click the bookmarklet, the browser sees a script from an untrusted, un-hashed source and simply refuses to run it.</li>
<li><strong>The &quot;Workaround&quot; is the Problem:</strong> The code in the bookmarklet that you want to rewrite is the very thing being blocked. You can&#39;t change the nature of the bookmarklet&#39;s execution to suddenly make it a trusted source.</li>
</ul>
<h3>What Are the Alternatives?</h3>
<p>Since you can&#39;t force the bookmarklet to work, you have to find a different way to run the code. Here are the most common and effective methods:</p>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Use Your Browser&#39;s Developer Console (Best Method):</strong>
This is the most direct and reliable way. The browser console is an environment specifically designed for developers to run scripts and debug code on a webpage. Scripts run in the console are generally not subject to the CSP of the page.</p>
<ul>
<li>Open the console (<strong>F12</strong> or <strong>Cmd+Option+I</strong> on Mac).</li>
<li>Paste the entire JavaScript code from the bookmarklet into the console.</li>
<li>Press <strong>Enter</strong>.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Turn it into a Browser Extension:</strong>
This is the most robust and permanent solution if you use the bookmarklet frequently on a specific site. Extensions run in their own secure &quot;isolated world&quot; and are not subject to the page&#39;s CSP.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>How it works:</strong> You would create a simple browser extension with a &quot;content script.&quot; This script would be configured to run automatically on the websites you want.</li>
<li><strong>Pros:</strong> It&#39;s a one-time setup, it runs automatically, and it&#39;s a completely secure and officially supported way to inject code.</li>
<li><strong>Cons:</strong> It&#39;s a bit more work than a bookmarklet and requires some basic knowledge of extension development.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Local Overrides in Chrome DevTools:</strong>
This is an advanced but very effective method for developers. You can use Chrome&#39;s &quot;Overrides&quot; feature to change the server&#39;s response headers locally, effectively removing the CSP.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>How it works:</strong> You &quot;override&quot; the page&#39;s response headers to remove the <code>Content-Security-Policy</code> header.</li>
<li><strong>Pros:</strong> You can make the bookmarklet work on a site with a strict CSP.</li>
<li><strong>Cons:</strong> This is a temporary, developer-only solution that requires manual setup for each site and is not practical for an everyday user.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>In summary: The &quot;rewrite&quot; isn&#39;t in the code itself, but in how you execute it.</strong> The original bookmarklet is fine; the problem is the secure environment it&#39;s trying to run in. The most user-friendly and reliable solution is to simply paste the code directly into the browser&#39;s developer console.</p>
<br></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--6-col mdl-typography--body-1 mdl-typography--text-right"></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col mdl-typography--caption"><b>Products:</b><br>&emsp;Gemini Apps<br><b>Why is this here?</b><br>&emsp;This activity was saved to your Google Account because the following settings were on:&nbsp;Gemini Apps Activity.&nbsp;You can control these settings &nbsp;<a href="https://myaccount.google.com/activitycontrols">here</a>.</div></div></div><div class="outer-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col mdl-shadow--2dp"><div class="mdl-grid"><div class="header-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col"><p class="mdl-typography--title">Gemini in Google Messages<br></p></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--6-col mdl-typography--body-1">Prompted Why wasn&#39;t that bookmarklet working<br>Deleting this prompt here will remove it from your Gemini Apps Activity, but not from Messages<br>Sep 11, 2025, 8:06:14 AM EDT<br><p>There are a few reasons why a JavaScript bookmarklet might not be working, and it can depend on the specific website and your browser&#39;s security settings.</p>
<p>Here are the most common culprits:</p>
<p><strong>1. Content Security Policy (CSP)</strong></p>
<p>This is the most likely reason. Many modern websites have a strong Content Security Policy, which is a security feature that tells the browser what kind of external resources (like scripts) are allowed to run on the page.</p>
<ul>
<li>The CSP might be configured to block inline scripts, which is exactly what a bookmarklet is.</li>
<li>It might also block scripts from a source that isn&#39;t on a pre-approved list.</li>
</ul>
<p>In this case, the browser is simply doing its job by blocking the code from running to protect you from potentially malicious scripts.</p>
<p><strong>2. The Website&#39;s Code Itself</strong></p>
<p>Some websites use JavaScript frameworks or security measures that can interfere with the bookmarklet&#39;s code.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Overwriting elements:</strong> The website&#39;s scripts might overwrite or remove the DOM elements that your bookmarklet is trying to create, like the <code>div</code> overlay.</li>
<li><strong>Conflicting styles:</strong> The website&#39;s CSS could have a high specificity rule that overrides the styles you&#39;re trying to apply to the overlay, making it invisible.</li>
<li><strong>JavaScript errors:</strong> If the bookmarklet&#39;s code has a syntax error or tries to access an element that doesn&#39;t exist, it will fail to execute properly.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>3. Browser or Device-Specific Issues</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Mobile browsers:</strong> Some mobile browsers, especially on iOS, have limitations or different ways of handling bookmarklets. They may not execute the code correctly or at all.</li>
<li><strong>Security settings:</strong> Your browser&#39;s security settings or extensions (like script blockers) might be preventing the code from running.</li>
<li><strong>Incorrect installation:</strong> You might have made a mistake when copying and pasting the code, such as including line breaks or extra spaces, which can break the single-line nature of a bookmarklet.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>How to Debug It</strong></p>
<p>The best way to figure out what&#39;s wrong is to use your browser&#39;s developer tools.</p>
<ol>
<li>
<p><strong>Open the Developer Console:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Right-click on the webpage and select &quot;Inspect&quot; or &quot;Inspect Element.&quot;</li>
<li>Go to the &quot;Console&quot; tab.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Look for Errors:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>After you click the bookmarklet, the console will likely show an error message if the code was blocked or failed to run.</li>
<li>Look for phrases like &quot;Content Security Policy,&quot; &quot;blocked by CSP,&quot; or other JavaScript error messages.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Paste the Code Directly:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Copy the entire bookmarklet code.</li>
<li>Paste it directly into the developer console and press Enter. If it works there but not as a bookmarklet, it&#39;s a strong sign that the CSP is the problem.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<br></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--6-col mdl-typography--body-1 mdl-typography--text-right"></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col mdl-typography--caption"><b>Products:</b><br>&emsp;Gemini Apps<br><b>Why is this here?</b><br>&emsp;This activity was saved to your Google Account because the following settings were on:&nbsp;Gemini Apps Activity.&nbsp;You can control these settings &nbsp;<a href="https://myaccount.google.com/activitycontrols">here</a>.</div></div></div><div class="outer-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col mdl-shadow--2dp"><div class="mdl-grid"><div class="header-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--12-col"><p class="mdl-typography--title">Gemini in Google Messages<br></p></div><div class="content-cell mdl-cell mdl-cell--6-col mdl-typography--body-1">Prompted javascript:(function(){try{const OP=0.15,SPEED=0.7,BLEND=&#39;screen&#39;,ID=&#39;__psy_tint_overlay__&#39;,BADGE=&#39;__psy_tint_badge__&#39;,existing=document.getElementById(ID);if(existing){const rid=Number(existing.dataset.raf)||0;if(rid)cancelAnimationFrame(rid);existing.remove();const b=document.getElementById(BADGE);if(b)b.remove();console.log(&#39;Psy tint stopped&#39;);return;}const overlay=document.createElement(&#39;div&#39;);overlay.id=ID;overlay.dataset.raf=&#39;0&#39;;overlay.dataset.opacity=String(OP);Object.assign(overlay.style,{position:&#39;fixed&#39;,top:&#39;0&#39;,left:&#39;0&#39;,width:&#39;100%&#39;,height:&#39;100%&#39;,pointerEvents:&#39;none&#39;,zIndex:&#39;2147483647&#39;,mixBlendMode:BLEND,transition:&#39;background 0.08s linear&#39;,willChange:&#39;background&#39;,opacity:&#39;1&#39;});const badge=document.createElement(&#39;div&#39;);badge.id=BADGE;badge.textContent=&#39;🌈&#39;;badge.title=&#39;Psychedelic tint active — click bookmark again to stop&#39;;Object.assign(badge.style,{position:&#39;fixed&#39;,right:&#39;8px&#39;,bottom:&#39;8px&#39;,zIndex:&#39;2147483647&#39;,fontSize:&#39;16px&#39;,padding:&#39;6px&#39;,borderRadius:&#39;6px&#39;,backdropFilter:&#39;blur(4px)&#39;,pointerEvents:&#39;none&#39;});(document.body||document.documentElement).appendChild(overlay);(document.body||document.documentElement).appendChild(badge);let hue=0;function frame(){const op=parseFloat(overlay.dataset.opacity)||OP;const h1=Math.round(hue%360);const h2=Math.round((hue+90)%360);overlay.style.background=&#39;linear-gradient(90deg, hsla(&#39;+h1+&#39;,100%,50%,&#39;+op+&#39;), hsla(&#39;+((h1+45)%360)+&#39;,100%,55%,&#39;+op+&#39;), hsla(&#39;+h2+&#39;,100%,50%,&#39;+op+&#39;))&#39;;hue=(hue+SPEED)%360;overlay.dataset.raf=String(requestAnimationFrame(frame));}frame();console.log(&#39;Psychedelic tint started (opacity &#39;+OP+&#39;)&#39;);}catch(e){console.error(&#39;Psy tint error&#39;,e);alert(&#39;Psy tint bookmarklet error: &#39;+e.message);} })();
<br>Deleting this prompt here will remove it from your Gemini Apps Activity, but not from Messages<br>Sep 11, 2025, 8:05:56 AM EDT<br><p>This code is a <strong>JavaScript bookmarklet</strong>.</p>
<p>When you click this bookmarklet in your browser, it creates a psychedelic, color-changing overlay on the current webpage.</p>
<p>Here&#39;s a breakdown of what the code does:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Checks for an existing overlay:</strong> It first checks if the overlay is already active. If it is, the code stops the animation and removes the overlay and a small &quot;🌈&quot; badge, effectively acting as a toggle.</li>
<li><strong>Creates elements:</strong> If the overlay isn&#39;t active, it creates a new <code>div</code> element for the overlay and another for the badge.</li>
<li><strong>Applies styles:</strong> It gives these elements a fixed position, full-screen coverage, and a high <code>z-index</code> so they appear on top of everything. The <code>mix-blend-mode</code> is set to <code>screen</code>, which creates the bright, vibrant color effect.</li>
<li><strong>Animates colors:</strong> It uses <code>requestAnimationFrame</code> to run a continuous loop. In each frame, it updates the <code>background</code> property of the overlay with a new gradient. The colors in the gradient (<code>hue</code>) are slowly shifted to create the flowing, psychedelic effect.</li>
<li><strong>Shows a badge:</strong> A small rainbow emoji badge is added to the bottom right of the screen to indicate that the effect is active.</li>
</ul>
<p>In short, this code is a fun little tool for adding a trippy visual effect to any webpage.</p>
